<worksheet>
    <title>Lab 6, Due: December ??, 2026</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <term>Instructions:</term> Complete the following exercises in your
            groups.
            For the write up:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Include descriptions of your work in Sage.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You only need to hand in one report for your group.
                        Only include the names of students who contributed.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        This lab is graded out of 30 points.
                        The number of points for each part are indicated next to
                        the problem.
                        You earn full credit by including your reasoning,
                        following all directions, and making only minor errors.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            Today we'll look at the linear algebra behind Google's wildly
            successful search engine.
            Back when Google used to tell you the number of search results,
            I googled the phrase <q>linear algebra.</q> Google tells me that it
            knows of about 170 <em>million</em> (!) pages that contain those two
            words, and that the Wikipedia page is likely to be useful to me.
            So how does it determine which ones to list first?
        </p>

        <p>
            According to Wikipedia, in 1996 Larry Page and Sergey Brin developed
            an algorithm at Stanford University as part of a research project
            about a new kind of search engine.
            As computer science PhD students they had the idea that information
            on the web could be ordered in a hierarchy by <q>link popularity</q>: a
            page ranks higher if there are more links to it.
            So basically Google's first algorithm computes a quantity called
            <em>PageRank</em> <trademark/> for each page on the Internet.
            Pages with a higher PageRank are deemed to be more valuable.
            The key to computing PageRank lies in an algorithm involving
            eigenvectors that we'll study in this lab.
        </p>

        <p>
            As a note, many of the linear algebra ideas needed for their
            algorithm to work were already discovered prior to 1996.
            Ideas of eigenvectors go back to the 18th century, and the
            Perron-Frobenius Theorem is from 1907 and 1912.
            Of course, Google now uses additional techniques, with many of them
            AI driven.
            AI techniques like machine learning also have their roots in linear
            algebra.
            (So, IMHO, it is always good to learn more mathematics.)
        </p>

        <p>
            While one could determine the importance of a web page by allowing
            people to vote for their favorite web pages, Google wants their
            rankings to be free of any human bias so it examines the structure
            of the Internet itself to rank pages.
            Here's the idea: if my home page is valuable, many other pages will
            link to it.
            To guard against gaming the rankings by creating lots of pages that
            link to my home page, Google views a page as being more valuable
            when valuable pages link to it.
            For instance, if three of my friends link to my home page, that
            means three people think I have a valuable page.
            But if <em>nytimes.com</em>, <em>amazon.com</em>, and
            <em>beyonce.com</em> link to me, that probably means more people
            will find my page to be valuable so I should have a higher PageRank.
        </p>

        <p>
            Before we begin, there are a few terms and ideas are required for
            this lab that build on earlier ideas in the course.
            Each of these will be referred to in some way in the lab that
            follows, so if you see an unfamiliar term, you can refer here to
            make sense of it.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A vector whose entries are nonnegative and add to 1 is
                        called a <em>probability vector</em>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        A square matrix whose columns are probability vectors is
                        called a <em>stochastic matrix</em>.
                        For example, the matrix
                        <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}0.9 &amp; 0.3 \\ 0.1 &amp; 0.7 \\\end{array}\right]</m>
                        is a stochastic matrix since each of its columns is a
                        probability vector.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        A <em>Markov chain</em> is a dynamical system
                        <m>\xvec_{k+1}= A\xvec_{k}</m> for which the matrix
                        <m>A</m> is a stochastic matrix.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        If a stochastic matrix has an eigenvector <m>\qvec</m>
                        with corresponding eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 1</m>, it
                        follows that <m>A \qvec = \qvec</m>, and we call
                        <m>\qvec</m> a <em>steady-state vector</em> (also known
                        as a <em>stationary vector</em>).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        We say that a matrix <m>A</m> is <em>positive</em> if
                        either <m>A</m> or some power <m>A^{k}</m> has all
                        positive entries.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Finally, a famous theorem (The Perron-Frobenius Theorem)
                        tells us some powerful fact about positive stochastic
                        matrices
                        <q>If <m>A</m> is a positive stochastic matrix then one eigenvalue of <m>A</m> is <m>\lambda = 1</m> with a corresponding eigenvector <m>\qvec</m> having all positive entries, and every other eigenvalue of <m>A</m> satisfies <m>|\lambda_{j}| &lt; 1</m>.</q>
                        This means that if <m>A</m> is a positive stochastic
                        matrix, the Markov chain <m>\xvec_{k+1}= A\xvec_{k}</m>
                        will converge to <m>\qvec</m> regardless of the initial
                        vector <m>\xvec_{0}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            Here's the basic idea for how to compute PageRank.
            Each webpage has a certain amount of PageRank that we denote by
            <m>x_{i}</m>.
            Each page divides its PageRank into equal portions, one for each
            outgoing link.
            Each page then gives one portion of its PageRank to each page it
            links to.
            A page's PageRank is then the sum of all the PageRank it receives
            from pages that link to it.
        </p>

        <p>
            One super valuable math skill is to consider a simpler problem.
            So, suppose that the entire internet is composed of only three web
            pages and the web pages contain links to one another as shown below.
        </p>

        <image source="images/lab6-cyclicweb.png" width="50%"/>


        <p>
            The PageRank of page <m>i</m> will be denoted by <m>x_{i}</m>.
            PageRank is both <q>given away</q> and <q>earned</q> by linking to
            other web pages.
            In particular,
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If web page <m>i</m> links to <m>m</m> other pages, then
                        its PageRank <m>x_{i}</m> is divided into <m>m</m> equal
                        units of <m>x_{i}/m</m>.
                        It therefore gives <m>x_{i}/m</m> to each of the pages
                        it links to.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The PageRank <m>x_{i}</m> is also the sum of all the
                        PageRanks it collects from the pages that link to it.
                        For instance, in the figure above,
                        <md>
                            x_{1} = x_{2} + \frac{1}{2} x_{3},
                        </md>
                        since Page 2 links only to Page 1 (Page 2 thus gives all
                        of its rank - <m>x_{2}</m> - to Page 1), while Page 3
                        gives half of its rank - <m>\frac{1}{2}x_{3}</m> - to
                        Page 1 since Page 3 links to both Page 1 and Page 2.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </introduction>

