    </exercise>

    <p>
        <em>Here is some more key perspective to read before answering subsequent questions</em>.
        This steady-state (or stationary) vector <m>\vec{x}</m> that satisfies
        <m>\xvec = G\xvec</m> is typically called the <q>PageRank vector</q>
        since the entries in it are the PageRanks of the various pages.
        It has also been called <q>The 25 Billion Dollar Eigenvector,</q> since
        it is the vector that made Google so much of its initial money.
    </p>

    <p>
        Any scalar multiple of <m>\vec{x}</m> is also a stationary vector (because
        if <m>A\vvec=\vvec</m> then <m>A(k\vvec) = k(A\vvec)=k\vvec</m>).
        When dealing with stochastic matrices, we usually require that the
        entries in <m>\vec{x}</m> add up to 1 since we think of them as
        probabilities.
        In this case, there is really no need to impose that restriction.
        What's important is the relative size of the numbers.
    </p>

    <p>
        Further, remember that Google is working with <em>the</em> Internet, the
        world-wide web which contains over a one billion websites and over 130
        trillion individual web pages.
        This means it is not feasible to compute the stationary vector by
        finding all of the solutions to <m>(G-I)\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.
        Instead, the Google PageRank algorithm creates a Markov chain and
        iterates.
        For instance, the algorithm begins with any initial guess for the
        PageRank, say,
        <md>
            \vec{x}_{0}=\left[\begin{array}{c}1 \\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ 0\end{array}\right]
        </md>
        and creates <m>\vec{x}_{1}=G\vec{x}_{0}</m>, <m>\vec{x}_{2}=G\vec{x}_{1}</m>,
        <m>\ldots</m>, and in general, <m>\vec{x}_{k+1}=G\vec{x}_{k}</m>.
        These vectors will typically
        <fn>Note that technically we need every entry in some power of the matrix <m>G</m> 
                    to be positive in order to guarantee convergence.</fn>
        converge to the PageRank vector.
        That is, as <m>k</m> gets large, <m>\vec{x}_{k} \to \vec{x}</m> due to the
        important theorem from more advanced linear algebra, the
        Perron-Frobenius Theorem, mentioned at the top of page 2 of this lab.
    </p>

