    <exercise>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    (3) Google fixes the problems shown in #'s 6 and 7 by
                    turning the matrix into a positive stochastic matrix.
                    If there are <m>n</m> pages on the internet, they construct
                    the matrix <m>H_{n}</m> which is <m>n\times n</m> and all of
                    the entries are <m>\frac{1}{n}</m>.
                    Then they create <m>G'</m> a positive stochastic matrix that
                    mixes the Google matrix and <m>H_{n}</m> together.
                    That is, <m>G' = \alpha G + (1-\alpha)H_{n}</m> for some
                    <m>\alpha</m>.
                    The weight <m>\alpha</m> they use is a trade secret, but
                    it's thought to be about 0.15.
                    For this problem, use the code at
                    <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/3dM"/>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

